[238.976242, "o", "grep: /app/docs/sed.txt: binary file matches\r\n379-\r\n380-     [addr]X[options]\r\n381-\r\n382-   X is a single-letter ‘sed’ command.  ‘[addr]’ is an optional line\r\n383-address.  If ‘[addr]’ is specified, the command X will be executed only\r\n384:on the matched lines.  ‘[addr]’ can be a single line number, a regular\r\n385-expression, or a range of lines (*note sed addresses::).  Additional\r\n386-‘[options]’ are used for some ‘sed’ commands.\r\n387-\r\n388-   The following example deletes lines 30 to 35 in the input.  ‘30,35’\r\n389-is an address range.  ‘d’ is the delete command:\r\n390-\r\n391-     sed '30,35d' input.txt > output.txt\r\n392-\r\n393-   The following example prints all input until a line starting with the\r\n394-string ‘foo’ is found.  If such line is found, ‘sed’ will terminate with\r\n395-exit status 42.  If such line was not found (and no other error\r\n396-occurred), ‘sed’ will exit with status 0.  ‘/^foo/’ is a\r\n397-regular-expression address.  ‘q’ is the quit comma"]
[238.976368, "o", "nd.  ‘42’ is the\r\n398-command option.\r\n399-\r\n400-     sed '/^foo/q42' input.txt > output.txt\r\n401-\r\n402-   Commands within a SCRIPT or SCRIPT-FILE can be separated by\r\n--\r\n578-\r\n579-‘{ CMD ; CMD ... }’\r\n580-     Group several commands together.\r\n581-\r\n582-‘=’\r\n583:     Print the current input line number (with a trailing newline).\r\n584-\r\n585-‘: LABEL’\r\n586-     Specify the location of LABEL for branch commands (‘b’, ‘t’, ‘T’).\r\n587-\r\n588-3.3 The ‘s’ Command\r\n589-===================\r\n590-\r\n591-The ‘s’ command (as in substitute) is probably the most important in\r\n592-‘sed’ and has a lot of different options.  The syntax of the ‘s’ command\r\n593-is ‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS’.\r\n594-\r\n595-   Its basic concept is simple: the ‘s’ command attempts to match the\r\n596-pattern space against the supplied regular expression REGEXP; if the\r\n597-match is successful, then that portion of the pattern space which was\r\n598-matched is replaced with REPLACEMENT.\r\n599-\r\n600-   Fo"]
[238.976375, "o", "r details about REGEXP syntax *note Regular Expression Addresses:\r\n601-Regexp Addresses.\r\n--\r\n833-\r\n834-     Instances of the ‘/’ (or whatever other character is used in its\r\n835-     stead), ‘\\’, or newlines can appear in the SOURCE-CHARS or\r\n836-     DEST-CHARS lists, provide that each instance is escaped by a ‘\\’.\r\n837-     The SOURCE-CHARS and DEST-CHARS lists _must_ contain the same\r\n838:     number of characters (after de-escaping).\r\n839-\r\n840-     See the ‘tr’ command from GNU coreutils for similar functionality.\r\n841-\r\n842-‘a TEXT’\r\n843-     Appending TEXT after a line.  This is a GNU extension to the\r\n844-     standard ‘a’ command - see below for details.\r\n845-\r\n846-     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line:\r\n847-          $ seq 3 | sed '2a hello'\r\n848-          1\r\n849-          2\r\n850-          hello\r\n851-          3\r\n852-\r\n853-     Leading whitespace after the ‘a’ command is ignored.  The text to\r\n854-     add is read until the end of the line.\r\n855-\r\n856-‘a\\"]
[238.976392, "o", "’\r\n--\r\n1002-          3\r\n1003-\r\n1004-          $ sed -e '2c\\' -e \"$VAR\"\r\n1005-\r\n1006-‘=’\r\n1007:     Print out the current input line number (with a trailing newline).\r\n1008-\r\n1009-          $ printf '%s\\n' aaa bbb ccc | sed =\r\n1010-          1\r\n1011-          aaa\r\n1012-          2\r\n1013-          bbb\r\n1014-          3\r\n1015-          ccc\r\n1016-\r\n1017-     As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.\r\n1018-\r\n1019-‘l N’\r\n1020-     Print the pattern space in an unambiguous form: non-printable\r\n1021-     characters (and the ‘\\’ character) are printed in C-style escaped\r\n1022-     form; long lines are split, with a trailing ‘\\’ character to\r\n1023-     indicate the split; the end of each line is marked with a ‘$’.\r\n1024-\r\n1025-     N specifies the desired line-wrap length; a length of 0 (zero)\r\n--\r\n1415-instance on each affected line.\r\n1416-\r\n1417-     sed 's/hello/world/' input.txt > output.txt\r\n1418-\r\n1419-   Addresses can contain regular expressions to match lines based on\r\n1420:"]
[238.976459, "o", "content instead of line numbers.  The following command replaces ‘hello’\r\n1421-with ‘world’ only on lines containing the string ‘apple’:\r\n1422-\r\n1423-     sed '/apple/s/hello/world/' input.txt > output.txt\r\n1424-\r\n1425-   An address range is specified with two addresses separated by a comma\r\n1426-(‘,’).  Addresses can be numeric, regular expressions, or a mix of both.\r\n1427-The following command replaces ‘hello’ with ‘world’ only on lines 4 to\r\n1428-17 (inclusive):\r\n1429-\r\n1430-     sed '4,17s/hello/world/' input.txt > output.txt\r\n1431-\r\n1432-   Appending the ‘!’ character to the end of an address specification\r\n1433-(before the command letter) negates the sense of the match.  That is, if\r\n1434-the ‘!’ character follows an address or an address range, then only\r\n1435-lines which do _not_ match the addresses will be selected.  The\r\n1436-following command replaces ‘hello’ with ‘world’ only on lines _not_\r\n1437-containing the string ‘apple’:\r\n1438-\r\n--\r\n1637-          "]
[238.976483, "o", "$ seq 10 | sed -n '6,+2p'\r\n1638-          6\r\n1639-          7\r\n1640-          8\r\n1641-\r\n1642:     ADDR1 can be a line number or a regular expression.\r\n1643-\r\n1644-‘ADDR1,~N’\r\n1645-     Matches ADDR1 and the lines following ADDR1 until the next line\r\n1646:     whose input line number is a multiple of N.  The following command\r\n1647-     prints starting at line 6, until the next line which is a multiple\r\n1648-     of 4 (i.e.  line 8):\r\n1649-\r\n1650-          $ seq 10 | sed -n '6,~4p'\r\n1651-          6\r\n1652-          7\r\n1653-          8\r\n1654-\r\n1655:     ADDR1 can be a line number or a regular expression.\r\n1656-\r\n1657-4.5 Zero Address\r\n1658-================\r\n1659-\r\n1660-As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, ‘0’ address can be used in two cases:\r\n1661-  1. In a regex range addresses as ‘0,/REGEXP/’ (*note Zero Address\r\n1662-     Regex Range::).\r\n1663-  2. With the ‘r’ command, inserting a file before the first line (*note\r\n1664-     Adding a header to multiple files::).\r\n1665-\r\n1666-   Note that these are"]
[238.976489, "o", " the only places where the ‘0’ address makes\r\n1667-sense; Commands which are given the ‘0’ address in any other way will\r\n1668-give an error.\r\n1669-\r\n1670-5 Regular Expressions: selecting text\r\n1671-*************************************\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@4c62d36b2fbb:/app# "]
