[239.334549, "o", "1705-alternatives and repetitions in the pattern.  These are encoded in the\r\n1706-pattern by the use of “special characters”, which do not stand for\r\n1707-themselves but instead are interpreted in some special way.\r\n1708-\r\n1709:   The character ‘^’ (caret) in a regular expression matches the\r\n1710-beginning of the line.  The character ‘.’ (dot) matches any single\r\n1711-character.  The following ‘sed’ command matches and prints lines which\r\n1712-start with the letter ‘b’, followed by any single character, followed by\r\n1713-the letter ‘d’:\r\n1714-\r\n1715-     $ printf \"%s\\n\" abode bad bed bit bid byte body | sed -n '/^b.d/p'\r\n1716-     bad\r\n1717-     bed\r\n1718-     bid\r\n1719-     body\r\n1720-\r\n1721-   The following sections explain the meaning and usage of special\r\n1722-characters in regular expressions.\r\n1723-\r\n--\r\n1966-=============================================\r\n1967-\r\n1968-A “bracket expression” is a list of characters enclosed by ‘[’ and ‘]’.\r\n1969-It matches any single c"]
[239.334575, "o", "haracter in that list; if the first character of\r\n1970:the list is the caret ‘^’, then it matches any character *not* in the\r\n1971-list.  For example, the following command replaces the strings ‘gray’ or\r\n1972-‘grey’ with ‘blue’:\r\n1973-\r\n1974-     sed  's/gr[ae]y/blue/'\r\n1975-\r\n1976-   Bracket expressions can be used in both *note basic: BRE syntax. and\r\n1977-*note extended: ERE syntax. regular expressions (that is, with or\r\n1978-without the ‘-E’/‘-r’ options).\r\n1979-\r\n1980-   Within a bracket expression, a “range expression” consists of two\r\n1981-characters separated by a hyphen.  It matches any single character that\r\n1982-sorts between the two characters, inclusive.  In the default C locale,\r\n1983-the sorting sequence is the native character order; for example, ‘[a-d]’\r\n1984-is equivalent to ‘[abcd]’.\r\n"]
